  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2512.14905},
  urldate = {2026-01-05},
  abstract = {The thermal emission at sub-millimeter wavelengths carries unique information in many astronomical applications ranging from disks and planet formation around young stars, to galaxy evolution studies at cosmological distances. Advancing on the mapping speed to detect this faint emission in ground-based astronomy has been a technical challenge for decades. The APEX Microwave Kinetic Inductance Detector (AMKID) camera was designed to accomplish this task. The instrument is a wide field-of-view camera based on kinetic inductance detectors. It is installed on the 12\textasciitilde meter APEX telescope in Chile at 5.100\textasciitilde meters above see level. The instrument operates dual color, covering simultaneously the 350\textasciitilde GHz and 850\textasciitilde GHz atmospheric windows. It has a large field-of-view of 15.3'x15.3', and an unprecedented number of pixels: 13.952\textasciitilde detectors in the high frequency band and 3.520\textasciitilde detectors in the low frequency band. Here we present a complete description of the instrument design and construction together with results of the successful low frequency array (LFA) commissioning campaign executed during the last year. The LFA performance is in good agreement with design parameters, with detector sensitivity of 2.2\textasciitilde mK\$\textbackslash sqrt\textbraceleft s\textbraceright\$ and diffraction limited beam sizes of 17.0''. On-sky measurements demonstrate a sensitivity of 70-90\textasciitilde mJy\$\textbackslash sqrt\textbraceleft s\textbraceright\$ per detector when operating under good atmospheric conditions (PWV below 1.0mm). With this performance the LFA regularly achieve a mapping sensitivity of 25\textasciitilde mJy when mapping a square degree in an hour. AMKID on APEX with its dual color observing capabilities, high sensitivity, large field-of-view and high angular resolution holds the promise to open a new range of science with the APEX telescope.},
